/**
	Logging over the build-selected provider.

	A `Logger` is an ordinary service handle: a `dyn mut` view of a sink, the same
	shape `fmt.Writer` has. Nothing about it is compiler-known. What the
	program selects is which one the package-level procedures below dispatch to,
	and `-log-level` decides which of them have a body at all.

	There is no scoped or package-local override of the program logger. A package
	that needs a different sink, captured test output, or request-specific fields
	takes a `Logger` parameter or keeps one in a state object.
*/
// design.md "Build-selected providers"; standard-library "`core:log`".
@(public)
package log;

import "core:fmt";
import "core:io";
import "core:mem";
import "core:thread";
import "core:unsafe";

// `Level` is contributed by the compiler rather than declared, so it and
// `LOKE_LOG_LEVEL` are one type: a `when` over `LOKE_LOG_LEVEL <= .Info`
// compares two values of the same enum, and a second nominal type here would
// make that comparison impossible to write.

/**
	The severity of a message, which is also `LOKE_LOG_LEVEL`'s type. `Off` is a
	threshold only, since nothing logs at it, and it is last so that
	`LOKE_LOG_LEVEL <= .Error` is false when logging is compiled out entirely.
*/
@(intrinsic)
Level :: enum { Debug, Info, Warning, Error, Off }

/**
	One logging sink. Any record with this `write` is one; the message carries no
	line ending.
*/
Sink :: interface($Self: type) {
	slot write: proc(self: inout Self, level: Level, message: string_view);
}

/**
	The handle a program logs through: a view of a sink that lives as long as the
	process, since a factory's result is kept for the life of the program.
*/
Logger :: dyn mut Sink;

// Where a selected logging provider is published. Generated startup writes the
// factory's result here, once, before `main`; until then it is `.none` and
// every call goes to the standard sink below. That is also what makes an
// unselected build need no initialization at all. Synchronized: it is
// published before any thread starts and never replaced, and the provider it
// names accepts concurrent records (design.md "Build-selected providers").
@(private, synchronized)
selected: Option(Logger) = .none;

/**
	The handle the program is logging through, for code that wants to pass it on
	rather than call the package procedures.
*/
current :: proc() -> Logger {
	return selected or_else standard_logger();
}

/**
	The standard logger: one line per record on the process error stream, with
	the level in front of it. It is the fallback *and* an ordinary provider — a
	build may select `log.standard_logger` explicitly to say so.
*/
// The view is made from a read-only address. `Standard` has no fields, so no
// write through the view reaches its storage, and the one thing its records
// share, the error stream, is serialized by `standard_lines` instead. A
// `&mut standard` would count as a write to a global every logging thread
// shares (design.md "Global write effects"), and warn at each `thread.spawn` of
// code that logs about a race that cannot happen.
standard_logger :: proc() -> Logger {
	return (Logger)((^mut Standard)(rawptr(&standard)));
}

@(private)
Standard :: struct {}

@(private)
standard: static Standard = {};

// Held for the whole of one standard record, so records from different threads
// never interleave within a line. Other writers to the error stream are not
// serialized against it.
@(private)
standard_lines: thread.Mutex(Unit);

impl Standard {
	write :: proc(self: inout, level: Level, message: string_view) {
		lines := standard_lines.lock();
		w := fmt.stderr();
		fmt.format_to(w, "[");
		fmt.format_to(w, level);
		fmt.format_to(w, "] ");
		fmt.format_to(w, message);
		fmt.format_to(w, "\n");
	}
}

/**
	A sink that writes the standard logger's lines, `[Level] message` and a
	newline, to an `io.Writer` it owns: an `fs.File`, a pipe, an
	`io.Buffer_Writer`. Each record is built in memory and handed to the writer
	whole, through one `io.write_all`, under a lock held for the whole record, so
	records from different threads never share a line.

	`Sink.write` has nothing to report through, so a record the writer refuses,
	in whole or in part, or that cannot be buffered, is dropped and counted;
	`dropped` answers the count. The sink keeps the buffer of its longest record
	for the next one.

	As a provider it lives in `static` storage, zero until the factory fills it:
	`sink = log.writer_sink(move(file)); return (log.Logger)(&mut sink);`.
*/
// standard-library "Writer sink".
// A provider's view is shared by every thread that logs, so `inout self` does
// not make a record exclusive: `records` does, held for the whole record and
// for reading `lost`, as `standard_lines` is for the standard logger. Every
// field is declared here so `into_writer` can destructure the sink, which
// needs no zero `W` to leave behind: a `dyn mut io.Writer` has none.
Writer_Sink :: struct($W: type) where io.Writer(W) {
	@(private) writer:  W,
	@(private) line:    io.Buffer_Writer,
	@(private) lost:    int,
	@(private) records: thread.Mutex(Unit),
}

/// A sink over `writer`, which it owns until `into_writer` gives it back.
writer_sink :: proc(writer: move $W) -> Writer_Sink(W) where io.Writer(W) {
	return Writer_Sink(W){writer = move(writer)};
}

impl Writer_Sink($W) {
	/// The `Sink` slot: one record, written whole or dropped and counted.
	write :: proc(self: inout, level: Level, message: string_view) where io.Writer(W) {
		held := self.records.lock();
		switch (put_line(inout self.writer, inout self.line, level, message)) {
		case .ok:
		case .err: self.lost += 1;
		}
	}

	/// How many records the writer refused or the sink could not buffer.
	dropped :: proc(self: ^) -> int {
		held := self.records.lock();
		return self.lost;
	}

	/**
		The writer back, for a caller that owns the sink: to close a file and see
		what `close` answers, or to read what a buffer collected.
	*/
	into_writer :: proc(self: move) -> W {
		writer, _, _, _ := move(self);
		return move(writer);
	}
}

// The whole line in memory first, so the writer sees one write per record: an
// unbuffered file then makes one system call, and an append handle shared with
// another process keeps the line together.
@(private)
put_line :: proc(
	writer: inout $W,
	line: inout io.Buffer_Writer,
	level: Level,
	message: string_view,
) -> Status(io.Error) where io.Writer(W) {
	line.clear();
	io.write_string(inout line, "[") or_return;
	io.write_string(inout line, level_name(level)) or_return;
	io.write_string(inout line, "] ") or_return;
	io.write_string(inout line, message) or_return;
	io.write_string(inout line, "\n") or_return;
	return io.write_all(inout writer, line.bytes());
}

// The spelling `fmt` gives a `Level`, which is what the standard logger
// prints, without formatting through a `fmt.Writer`.
@(private)
level_name :: proc(level: Level) -> string_view {
	switch (level) {
	case .Debug:   return "Debug";
	case .Info:    return "Info";
	case .Warning: return "Warning";
	case .Error:   return "Error";
	case .Off:     return "Off";
	}
}

// The four record procedures. Each guards its own body with the compiled level,
// so a call below it never reaches the provider.
//
// Discarding a callee's body does not discard the caller's expressions: an
// unguarded call below the level still checks and evaluates its arguments. To
// remove those too, guard the *call*:
//
//     when (LOKE_LOG_LEVEL <= .Debug) { log.debug("state", expensive()); }

debug :: proc(args: ..dyn fmt.Formattable) {
	when (LOKE_LOG_LEVEL <= Level.Debug) { record(Level.Debug, ..args); }
}

info :: proc(args: ..dyn fmt.Formattable) {
	when (LOKE_LOG_LEVEL <= Level.Info) { record(Level.Info, ..args); }
}

warning :: proc(args: ..dyn fmt.Formattable) {
	when (LOKE_LOG_LEVEL <= Level.Warning) { record(Level.Warning, ..args); }
}

error :: proc(args: ..dyn fmt.Formattable) {
	when (LOKE_LOG_LEVEL <= Level.Error) { record(Level.Error, ..args); }
}

/**
	Is anything at this level compiled in? A caller with an expensive argument
	asks the constant directly in a `when`; this is for a runtime branch over a
	level held in a variable. `Off` answers false.
*/
// The caller with a level in a variable can be handed `Off` from
// configuration, so `Off` answers false rather than comparing: it is a
// threshold, there is no procedure that records at it, and nothing logs there.
enabled :: proc(level: Level) -> bool {
	return level != Level.Off && LOKE_LOG_LEVEL <= level;
}

/**
	A record at a level chosen at runtime, which is what `enabled` above is for:
	having branched on a level held in a variable, this is how a caller records
	at it without switching over five members to reach one of the four fixed
	procedures. An `off` build discards it too.
*/
// The `when` is the same guard those four carry, asked once for the whole
// package: `<= Error` is false only when logging is compiled out entirely, so
// an `off` build discards this body too. `enabled` then does the per-level
// filtering that a compiled constant cannot, because the level is not one.
at :: proc(level: Level, args: ..dyn fmt.Formattable) {
	when (LOKE_LOG_LEVEL <= Level.Error) {
		if (enabled(level)) { record(level, ..args); }
	}
}

// The allocation here applies the library's ordinary failure policy, which for
// logging is worth stating rather than assuming: `fmt.to_string` panics when it
// cannot allocate, so a record emitted while handling a failure can end the
// process instead of reporting it. A log call is not a no-op under memory
// pressure. Nothing quieter is available while `core:fmt` has no `try_to_string`
// and these four procedures return nothing to report through.
//
// ponytail: one allocation per emitted record, because formatting into a
// caller-supplied buffer needs a `fmt.Writer` over storage this package would
// have to size arbitrarily, and truncation there would have to cut a rune. If
// logging volume makes this matter, give `Sink` a bytes `write` like
// `fmt.Sink`'s and stream into it instead. A suppressed call allocates
// nothing: its whole body is discarded.
@(private)
record :: proc(level: Level, args: ..dyn fmt.Formattable) {
	handle := current();
	text := fmt.to_string(mem.default_allocator(), ..args);
	handle.write(level, text);
}
